1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,359
Five outages. Five different error messages.

2
00:00:03,909 --> 00:00:07,706
Today you break a small network five ways, and fix every one of them.

3
00:00:08,256 --> 00:00:13,397
A name, a port, a firewall, a certificate, and a route. Each one leaves its own clue.

4
00:00:13,947 --> 00:00:17,766
Learn to read the clue, and you can find the fault in minutes, not hours.

5
00:00:19,816 --> 00:00:24,509
Here is the method. Every failure arrives as a message. Read the exact words.

6
00:00:25,059 --> 00:00:29,113
Could not resolve host. That is the name. D N S has no answer.

7
00:00:29,663 --> 00:00:33,972
Connection refused. The machine answered, but nothing is listening on that port.

8
00:00:34,522 --> 00:00:39,066
Timed out. Nobody answered at all. Often a firewall is dropping the packets.

9
00:00:39,616 --> 00:00:44,395
Certificate problem. The connection worked, but T L S does not trust the server.

10
00:00:44,945 --> 00:00:48,955
Network is unreachable. Your own machine has no route to get there.

11
00:00:49,505 --> 00:00:50,784
We meet each one live.

12
00:00:50,784 --> 00:00:53,166
I broke the lab in advance with a script,

13
00:00:53,166 --> 00:00:54,386
so you see the fault,

14
00:00:54,386 --> 00:00:54,909
the clue,

15
00:00:54,909 --> 00:00:55,607
and the fix.

16
00:00:57,657 --> 00:01:08,729
Fault one: the name. A page will not open. Type: curl -sS -m 3 http://shop.lab.test:8080

17
00:01:08,859 --> 00:01:10,261
Could not resolve host.

18
00:01:10,261 --> 00:01:12,455
The request never left this machine,

19
00:01:12,455 --> 00:01:14,406
because the name has no address.

20
00:01:14,956 --> 00:01:20,353
Ask D N S directly. Type: dig +short shop.lab.test

21
00:01:20,483 --> 00:01:25,112
Nothing comes back. The name is not in the zone: a typo, or a missing record.

22
00:01:25,662 --> 00:01:31,017
Compare with a name that exists. Type: dig +short web.lab.test

23
00:01:31,147 --> 00:01:34,255
That one returns ten dot zero dot two dot two.

24
00:01:34,255 --> 00:01:35,268
So D N S works,

25
00:01:35,268 --> 00:01:37,632
and the record for shop is missing.

26
00:01:38,182 --> 00:01:39,384
The fix is a record,

27
00:01:39,384 --> 00:01:40,465
or the right name.

28
00:01:40,465 --> 00:01:41,727
Check the name first,

29
00:01:41,727 --> 00:01:44,070
because every other test depends on it.

30
00:01:46,120 --> 00:01:56,957
Fault two: the port. The name works now. Type: curl -sS -m 3 http://web.lab.test:8080

31
00:01:57,087 --> 00:01:58,165
Failed to connect,

32
00:01:58,165 --> 00:01:59,541
after two milliseconds.

33
00:01:59,541 --> 00:02:00,918
That speed is the clue.

34
00:02:00,918 --> 00:02:02,534
Something answered at once,

35
00:02:02,534 --> 00:02:03,253
and said no.

36
00:02:03,803 --> 00:02:09,051
Go to the server, and ask who is listening. Type: sv ss -ltn

37
00:02:09,181 --> 00:02:10,988
Port eighty eighty is missing.

38
00:02:10,988 --> 00:02:13,518
Only eighty four forty three is listening.

39
00:02:13,518 --> 00:02:15,325
The web server is not running.

40
00:02:15,875 --> 00:02:18,691
Start it again. Type: sv startweb

41
00:02:18,821 --> 00:02:27,098
Try the page once more. Type: curl -s http://web.lab.test:8080

42
00:02:27,228 --> 00:02:31,964
Hello from the lab. Refused means the machine is fine, and the service is not.

43
00:02:34,014 --> 00:02:37,367
Fault three: the firewall.

44
00:02:37,367 --> 00:02:38,656
Same page,

45
00:02:38,656 --> 00:02:39,946
same port.

46
00:02:39,946 --> 00:02:45,619
Type: curl -sS -m 3 http://web.lab.test:8080

47
00:02:46,749 --> 00:02:52,147
Watch the difference. It was not refused. It waited three seconds, and gave up. Timed out.

48
00:02:52,697 --> 00:02:57,795
Nobody said no. The packets just vanished. That is what a firewall drop looks like.

49
00:02:58,345 --> 00:03:03,828
Is the server alive? Type: ping -c 1 10.0.2.2

50
00:03:03,958 --> 00:03:08,950
It replies, so the machine is up, and the path is fine. Only this one port is blocked.

51
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:15,559
On the server, list the firewall rules. Type: sv iptables -S INPUT

52
00:03:15,689 --> 00:03:21,406
There it is. A rule for T C P, destination port eighty eighty, with the action drop.

53
00:03:21,956 --> 00:03:27,353
Delete rule number one. Type: sv iptables -D INPUT 1

54
00:03:27,483 --> 00:03:35,654
Try the page again. Type: curl -s http://web.lab.test:8080

55
00:03:35,784 --> 00:03:36,970
Refused is instant.

56
00:03:36,970 --> 00:03:38,093
A timeout is slow.

57
00:03:38,093 --> 00:03:39,279
Remember that pair,

58
00:03:39,279 --> 00:03:42,461
and you can tell a stopped service from a firewall.

59
00:03:44,511 --> 00:03:46,162
Fault four: T L S.

60
00:03:46,162 --> 00:03:47,996
Now the secure page,

61
00:03:47,996 --> 00:03:50,931
on port eighty four forty three.

62
00:03:50,931 --> 00:03:54,232
Type: get https://web.lab.test:8443.

63
00:03:54,232 --> 00:03:56,984
Get is my short name for curl,

64
00:03:56,984 --> 00:03:59,552
keeping only the first line,

65
00:03:59,552 --> 00:04:02,303
because T L S errors are long.

66
00:04:02,853 --> 00:04:06,557
Certificate problem: unable to get local issuer certificate.

67
00:04:06,557 --> 00:04:07,545
The name worked,

68
00:04:07,545 --> 00:04:08,780
and the port worked.

69
00:04:08,780 --> 00:04:09,829
The trust failed.

70
00:04:10,379 --> 00:04:13,008
Read the certificate itself.

71
00:04:13,008 --> 00:04:15,731
Type: cert web.lab.test:8443.

72
00:04:15,731 --> 00:04:21,366
That is a short name I made for a longer open S S L command.

73
00:04:21,916 --> 00:04:24,399
The subject is web dot lab dot test,

74
00:04:24,399 --> 00:04:25,778
so the name matches.

75
00:04:25,778 --> 00:04:27,641
The issuer is Lab Root C A,

76
00:04:27,641 --> 00:04:30,952
a private authority this computer does not know.

77
00:04:30,952 --> 00:04:32,883
The dates cover a full year,

78
00:04:32,883 --> 00:04:34,332
so it is not expired.

79
00:04:34,882 --> 00:04:46,850
Tell curl to trust that authority. Type: get --cacert ca.pem https://web.lab.test:8443

80
00:04:46,980 --> 00:04:49,604
Hello from the lab. Trusted, and encrypted.

81
00:04:50,154 --> 00:04:54,952
A second mistake: the right certificate,

82
00:04:54,952 --> 00:04:57,231
but the wrong name.

83
00:04:57,231 --> 00:05:00,110
Use the address instead.

84
00:05:00,110 --> 00:05:05,748
Type: get --cacert ca.pem https://10.0.2.2:8443

85
00:05:05,878 --> 00:05:09,011
No alternative certificate subject name matches.

86
00:05:09,011 --> 00:05:11,296
A certificate is issued for a name,

87
00:05:11,296 --> 00:05:12,536
not for an address.

88
00:05:12,536 --> 00:05:14,625
Use the name on the certificate.

89
00:05:15,175 --> 00:05:17,269
You will see advice to add dash k.

90
00:05:17,269 --> 00:05:18,439
It skips the check.

91
00:05:18,439 --> 00:05:21,456
It hides the very problem you are trying to find,

92
00:05:21,456 --> 00:05:23,303
so never use it in production.

93
00:05:25,353 --> 00:05:33,950
Fault five: the route. Now even ping fails. Type: ping -c 1 -W 1 10.0.2.2

94
00:05:34,080 --> 00:05:39,392
Network is unreachable. Not a timeout. This machine already knows it has no way out.

95
00:05:39,942 --> 00:05:42,822
Look at the routing table. Type: ip route

96
00:05:42,952 --> 00:05:48,499
One line only: my own network. There is no default route, so nothing outside has a path.

97
00:05:49,049 --> 00:05:57,070
Add the default route, through the gateway. Type: sudo ip route add default via 10.0.1.1

98
00:05:57,200 --> 00:06:03,643
Ping again. Type: ping -c 1 -W 1 10.0.2.2

99
00:06:03,773 --> 00:06:04,351
No reply,

100
00:06:04,351 --> 00:06:06,277
and this time it is a timeout,

101
00:06:06,277 --> 00:06:07,305
not unreachable.

102
00:06:07,305 --> 00:06:08,910
The first fault is fixed.

103
00:06:08,910 --> 00:06:10,322
There is a second one.

104
00:06:10,872 --> 00:06:19,790
Where does the path stop? Type: traceroute -n -w 1 -q 1 -m 3 10.0.2.2

105
00:06:19,920 --> 00:06:24,314
Stars from the very first hop. Even the gateway is not passing traffic on.

106
00:06:24,864 --> 00:06:31,670
Ask the router. Type: rt sysctl net.ipv4.ip_forward

107
00:06:31,800 --> 00:06:42,829
Zero. Forwarding is switched off. Switch it on. Type: rt sysctl -w net.ipv4.ip_forward=1

108
00:06:42,959 --> 00:06:50,170
Ping one last time. Type: ping -c 1 -W 1 10.0.2.2

109
00:06:50,300 --> 00:06:53,926
Two faults, found one at a time, just by reading each message.

110
00:06:55,976 --> 00:06:59,005
Keep this table. It is the whole lesson in six rows.

111
00:06:59,555 --> 00:07:02,435
Could not resolve host: name. Check with dig.

112
00:07:02,985 --> 00:07:06,057
Connection refused: no listener. Check with s s.

113
00:07:06,607 --> 00:07:09,274
Timed out: a firewall drop. Check the rules.

114
00:07:09,824 --> 00:07:14,496
Certificate problem: trust, name, or dates. Check with open S S L.

115
00:07:15,046 --> 00:07:18,459
Network is unreachable: no route. Check I P route.

116
00:07:19,009 --> 00:07:23,041
Stars from hop one: a router not forwarding. Check with trace route.

117
00:07:25,091 --> 00:07:26,520
Watch out for four myths.

118
00:07:27,070 --> 00:07:29,120
Myth one: add dash k and move on.

119
00:07:29,120 --> 00:07:31,605
That switches the certificate check off.

120
00:07:31,605 --> 00:07:34,089
It hides the fault instead of fixing it.

121
00:07:34,639 --> 00:07:38,069
Myth two: refused and timed out mean the same thing.

122
00:07:38,069 --> 00:07:39,718
Refused is an instant no.

123
00:07:39,718 --> 00:07:41,103
A timeout is silence.

124
00:07:41,653 --> 00:07:43,748
Myth three: if the name resolves,

125
00:07:43,748 --> 00:07:44,954
the page will load.

126
00:07:44,954 --> 00:07:46,795
An address is only the start.

127
00:07:46,795 --> 00:07:49,397
The port and the firewall can still fail.

128
00:07:49,947 --> 00:07:52,515
Myth four: change several things at once.

129
00:07:52,515 --> 00:07:53,579
Change one thing,

130
00:07:53,579 --> 00:07:53,892
test,

131
00:07:53,892 --> 00:07:55,771
and only then change the next.

132
00:07:56,321 --> 00:07:57,622
One fault at a time.

133
00:07:59,672 --> 00:08:01,997
Here is how you check your own understanding.

134
00:08:02,547 --> 00:08:05,513
Tell refused from timed out, and say what each means.

135
00:08:06,063 --> 00:08:08,751
Check a name with dig, and a listener with s s.

136
00:08:09,301 --> 00:08:12,437
Read a certificate: the name, the issuer, and the dates.

137
00:08:12,987 --> 00:08:15,909
Read I P route, and spot a missing default route.

138
00:08:16,459 --> 00:08:18,657
Use trace route to see where a path stops.

139
00:08:19,207 --> 00:08:22,940
Fix a fault, changing one thing at a time, and test after each.

140
00:08:23,490 --> 00:08:25,111
To pass, you need all six.

141
00:08:27,161 --> 00:08:27,717
Your turn,

142
00:08:27,717 --> 00:08:29,552
in the virtual machine you built.

143
00:08:29,552 --> 00:08:32,110
Only break things in your own virtual machine.

144
00:08:32,660 --> 00:08:38,655
One: run curl against a made-up name, like shop dot example dot invalid. Read the message.

145
00:08:39,205 --> 00:08:41,378
Two: run curl against local host,

146
00:08:41,378 --> 00:08:42,958
port nine thousand nine.

147
00:08:42,958 --> 00:08:44,406
Nothing listens there.

148
00:08:44,406 --> 00:08:45,328
Is it instant?

149
00:08:45,878 --> 00:08:51,555
Three: start a server with python three dash m h t t p dot server eight thousand eighty,

150
00:08:51,555 --> 00:08:53,814
and curl it from a second terminal.

151
00:08:54,364 --> 00:08:59,676
Four: block that port with sudo I P tables, watch curl time out, then delete the rule.

152
00:09:00,226 --> 00:09:06,306
Five: read a real certificate, with open S S L s client, against example dot com.

153
00:09:06,856 --> 00:09:09,586
Six: write down your gateway from I P route,

154
00:09:09,586 --> 00:09:11,137
remove the default route,

155
00:09:11,137 --> 00:09:12,626
see network unreachable,

156
00:09:12,626 --> 00:09:13,618
and add it back.

157
00:09:14,168 --> 00:09:17,475
Mark yourself on the rubric. Pause the video, and do it now.

158
00:09:33,025 --> 00:09:33,900
Quick check.

159
00:09:34,450 --> 00:09:37,650
Which message means a firewall may be dropping packets?

160
00:09:38,200 --> 00:09:40,120
What does connection refused tell you?

161
00:09:40,670 --> 00:09:42,803
Which command checks that a name resolves?

162
00:09:43,353 --> 00:09:45,081
Why is dash k a bad fix?

163
00:09:45,631 --> 00:09:49,812
Objective covered: MC03-O08.

164
00:09:50,362 --> 00:09:52,432
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

165
00:10:02,982 --> 00:10:07,120
Next: your mastery scenario, where you fix a broken network on your own.

166
00:10:07,670 --> 00:10:10,998
If this helped, like, share and subscribe, so you do not miss it.
